WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:05.049
The credits for "The Batman" call him the
"Unseen Arkham Prisoner" – but real fans

00:00:05.049 --> 00:00:07.120
know where they've seen him before.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:09.910
Warning: spoilers for "The Batman" ahead.

00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:14.120
Teasing a villain for the next movie at the
end of the current one is, at this point,

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:16.650
standard operating procedure for comic book
movies.

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:20.900
The most recent "Batman" movie, "The Batman,"
includes just such a scene.

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:25.570
It's set in Arkham Asylum — Gotham City's
notoriously insecure institute for the "criminally

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:26.570
insane."

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:30.700
The scene in question features an exchange
between Paul Dano's Riddler and a familiar

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:34.020
murderous clown played by rising star Barry
Keoghan.

00:00:34.020 --> 00:00:38.370
Most audience members will tell you that the
scene is a clear setup for a "Batman" sequel,

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:42.780
one that once again sets the Dark Knight on
a collision course with the Clown Prince of

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:43.780
Crime.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:48.310
But there's more to this brief exchange between
two super-criminals than might immediately

00:00:48.310 --> 00:00:49.620
meet the eye.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:53.380
Director Matt Reeves revealed in an interview
published in conjunction with the film's release

00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:57.340
that he intended for the moment to have a
thematic significance within "The Batman"

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:01.470
itself, rather than functioning as a glorified
advertisement for future films.

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:02.880
"New friend of yours?"

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:04.399
"I'm not so sure."

00:01:04.399 --> 00:01:08.640
In a YouTube interview with IGN, director
Matt Reeves provided some insight into the

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:12.850
Barry Keoghan Joker scene in "The Batman,"
linking its function within the film to some

00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:14.890
of the movie's overarching themes.

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:18.670
Reeves explained that in his initial cut of
the movie, he removed the Arkham sequence

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:23.390
between Riddler and Joker, thinking it unimportant
to the movie's overall story.

00:01:23.390 --> 00:01:27.620
But when he did so, the stakes within another,
more crucial sequence — a scene later on

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:30.750
between Catwoman and Batman — were diminished.

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:34.119
Catwoman and Batman care about each other,
but at the end of the day, they're working

00:01:34.119 --> 00:01:35.670
on opposite sides of the law.

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:40.060
They're also divided by their attitudes towards
Gotham City — while Batman clings to a desperate

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:45.070
hope that his crime-fighting efforts can somehow
make a difference in the grand scheme of things,

00:01:45.070 --> 00:01:49.409
Catwoman is of the opinion that the city will
"never change," and that each quelled threat

00:01:49.409 --> 00:01:52.010
will ultimately be replaced by a new one.

00:01:52.010 --> 00:01:53.550
Remind you of anything?

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:57.990
In "The Batman," audiences already understand
the grim truth of Catwoman's words, because

00:01:57.990 --> 00:02:00.490
they've just seen the Joker himself enter
the picture.

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:03.650
How can Gotham ever change when that guy's
on the scene?

00:02:03.650 --> 00:02:08.340
The Joker scene, then, is a potent illustration
of the seemingly limitless number of threats

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:12.489
to the public's health and well-being that
the numerous villains of Gotham City will

00:02:12.489 --> 00:02:13.879
always present.

00:02:13.879 --> 00:02:19.010
This may be the Riddler's movie, but the Riddler
is far from the only villain in Gotham.

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:21.950
He's just one of them – and now he has a
new friend.

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:25.139
"If this continues, it won't be long until
you have nothing left."

00:02:25.139 --> 00:02:29.150
Matt Reeves shared some additional insight
about Barry Keoghan's scene in "The Batman"

00:02:29.150 --> 00:02:33.280
in an interview with Variety published the
day after his conversation with IGN.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:37.969
Reeves explained that originally, there was
another scene with Keoghan's "unseen Arkham

00:02:37.969 --> 00:02:42.920
prisoner," which saw Batman go and visit the
detainee, who greeted him like an old friend.

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:47.079
The deleted scene, which Reeves hopes to show
viewers one day, is said to have a similar

00:02:47.079 --> 00:02:52.569
vibe to the scenes between Clarice Starling
and Hannibal Lecter in "The Silence of the

00:02:52.569 --> 00:02:53.569
Lambs."

00:02:53.569 --> 00:02:54.569
"Hello Clarice.

00:02:54.569 --> 00:02:57.829
It's good to see you again."

00:02:57.829 --> 00:03:02.299
In addition to lending thematic weight to
Catwoman's philosophical outlook on Gotham

00:03:02.299 --> 00:03:06.370
City, Reeves explained that Keoghan's Joker
was likewise featured in order to accentuate

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:11.279
the preponderance of eccentric characters
in Gotham and further develop the film's central

00:03:11.279 --> 00:03:12.279
location.

00:03:12.279 --> 00:03:13.279
The director said,

00:03:13.279 --> 00:03:17.410
"I thought it'd be really neat if so much
of the fabric of Gotham just already existed,

00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:22.030
and it was like an old Warner Bros. gangster
movie, and if you took a certain turn, you

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:24.139
might see a character in his origins."

00:03:24.139 --> 00:03:28.620
Furthermore, the implied threat the Joker
poses to Gotham city is meant to reinforce

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:31.760
the fact that Batman's work is forever ongoing.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:36.169
Reeves explained that the scene's inclusion
helped audiences, quote, "see that Gotham

00:03:36.169 --> 00:03:39.079
was still Gotham, and that Batman really didn't
have a choice.

00:03:39.079 --> 00:03:41.129
He has to keep doing what he's gonna do."

00:03:41.129 --> 00:03:45.029
According to Reeves, whether or not Keoghan
reprises his role as the Joker in a future

00:03:45.029 --> 00:03:47.269
film has yet to be determined.

00:03:47.269 --> 00:03:51.859
But at the very least, Reeves has made it
clear that his scene in "The Batman" serves

00:03:51.859 --> 00:03:56.389
a clear purpose, whether he shows up again
in a future movie or not.

